In article <3595@gmuvax2.gmu.edu>, rauletta@gmuvax2.gmu.edu (R. J. Auletta) writes:
> 
> We are in possession of a Fujitsu Eagle Disk Drive

This is probably a single ported SMD drive. A second port card can be added to
make it a dual port drive. 

Get a popular good card that can also do the very latest/fastest/biggest SMD
drives should you ever get any. We have used the Dilog DQ256 with these, and
have had them dual ported so they can be alternately accessable to 2 different
machines. 

That Dilog controller optionally will format the drive into up
to 4 seperate drives (all of equal size). You could mount some from a controller
on one port, and the rest from the other. We have done that between an MV2, and 
a Unibus PDP11 (Dilog makes a U bus version of the card - DU256- that is other
wise identical).

There is a very real chance running LAVC on VMS 5.3 or better that you could
MOU the same drive from each of 2 machines and not have to serve it to the 
other over ethernet. The cluster s/w keeps everything clean. I havn't tried it
though.

Ribbon cables are fine, but if you really feel you MUST be totally FCC *pure*
there are expensive shielded round cables that still terminate in the same Berg
connectors. Dilog or Emulex or Andromeda or whoever makes your card(s) can help.

Think carefully about whether you want to sink $s into SMD hardware. True, the
latest SMD drives are BIG and FAST (and you will need the LATEST SMD 
controllers to run them), but 5 1/4 SCSI drives are also VERY fast (11 ms 
access) and have 150k - 200k hour MTBF ratings. You can hang 7 on a controller,
6 if you have 2 controllers each in a different VAX, and can mix DAT or 8mm
or CDROMs all on the same cable. Soon those 6 or 7 drives with some new
adapters will be 6 or 7 new 7 port SCSI sub cables (42 or 49 drives total).

You can even buy SCSI controllers that implement 2 SCSI cables. Try
CMD first, then there the others that do SCSI - Dilog, Emulex, TD Systems, etc.

You can buy SCSI controllers that automatically make 2 drives a shadow set
giving you 1) reliability, and 2) ~2x read throughput.

The new SCSI controllers are not as expensive as new SMD ones.

SCSI drives move quite freely to many different machines, SMD drives, well...

It just depends on where you want to put your money. Sometimes it is better 
to sell something FAST while it still has a little value. 

If you have NO future SMD plans, and can get a *bargain* on a used older 
